\chapter{Profiling (perf)} \label{c:profiler-perf}
%HEVEA\cutname{profiler-perf.html}

\section{s:profiler-perf}{Overview and usage}

This chapter describes how to profile OCaml programs using the Linux Profiling tool, perf(1). Perf is a multi-purpose tool included with Linux for profiling, tracing and scripting, and is the interface for the kernel perf_events observability subsystem. The main features that will be covered here are perf-record(1) for recording events and perf-report(1) for printing and visualising recorded events. Perf has many other features for profiling and visualising performance on  Linux, See (man perf(1), \href{https://perfwiki.github.io/main/}{perfwiki.github.io} or \href{https://www.brendangregg.com/perf.html}{https://www.brendangregg.com/perf.html}) for more general documentation.

\section{s:ocamlperf-compiling}{Compiling for profiling}

How to setup your executable for time profiling depends on which version of OCaml is being used. For OCaml versions 4.14 and earlier there is no special requirements for capturing traces. For OCaml 5.0 onwards, it is recommended to recompile with Frame Pointers enabled.

To use Frame Pointers, you must configure the compiler with --enable-frame-pointers. You can also
install an opam switch with Frame Pointers enabled as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
opam switch create <YOUR-SWITCH-NAME-HERE> ocaml-option-fp
\end{verbatim}

Frame Pointer support for OCaml is currently available for the x86_64 architecture, on Linux from OCaml 4.12 onwards, on macOS from OCaml 5.3, and for the arm64 architecture on Linux and macOS from OCaml 5.3. Other Tier-1 architecures POWER, riscv and s390x are currently unsupported.

OCaml 5 introduced non-contiguous stacks as part of the implementation of effects, mentioned in the PLDI 2021 paper on retrofitting effect handlers -- Section 5.5 in \href{Retrofitting effect handlers onto OCaml}{https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3453483.3454039}. The non-contiguous stacks do not work with the copying nature of perf, so traces produced for OCaml 5 without frame pointers enabled will be truncated and/or inaccurate. Further more they will also be truncated if your Linux distribution doesn't enable frame pointers for libraries OCaml links against (e.g. libc).

The `perf record` command, used for CPU profiling, works by copying (a segment) of the call stack at every sample and recording this into a perf.data file. These samples are then processed (using `perf report` to reconstruct the profiled program's call stack at every sample) offline, after recording has finished.

`perf record --call-graph` has multiple options for record call graphs:
\begin{itemize}
\item Frame Pointers, which is the default
\item DWARF's Call Frame Information (CFI)
\item Hardware Last Branch Record (LBR) available on certain Intel CPUs
\end{itemize}

* Explain name mangling rules

Perf will use the symbols present in an OCaml executable, so it is useful to understand OCaml's name mangling scheme to map these names to OCaml source locations. 

Before 5.1, ocamlopt mangles names using the following format `camlModule__identifier_stamp` while
5.1 onwards replaces the double underscore separator with a dot camlModule.identifier_stamp. Both options
are supported in perf.

For instance, consider the following program:
\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
module Compute = struct
  let rec fib n = 
    if n = 0 then 0
    else if n = 1 then 1
    else fib (n-1) + fib (n-2)
end

let main () = 
  let r = Compute.fib 20 in 
  Printf.printf "fib(20) = %d" r

let _ = main ()
\end{caml_example*}

Will produce the following names `camlFib.main_274` for `main` function and `camlFib.fib_271` for the `fib` function in the `Compute` module when compiled with OCaml 5.2.

The basic `perf` command is:
\begin{verbatim}
$ perf record -F 99 --call-graph fp <YOUR_EXECUTABLE>
\end{verbatim}

The `-F 99` tells perf to sample at 99Hz, which avoids generating excessive data for longer runs and is
unlikely to be in lockstep with other periodic activities. The `--call-graph fp` instructs perf to use
frame pointer to get the call-graph (which is the default anyway) and then whatever OCaml executable you
want to profile. This will create a `perf.data` file in the current directory, alternatively use `--output`
to choose a more descriptive filename.

This profile data can then be processed by `perf-record` in any number of ways, like \href{https://www.brendangregg.com/flamegraphs.html}{Flame Graphs} or online tools like \href{https://www.speedscope.app}{www.speedscope.app} or \href{https://profiler.firefox.com/}{profiler.firefox.com} or any other tool that accepts Linux perf data.

Additionally some perf tools (e.g. perf-report(1) and perf-annotate(1)) make use of DWARF debugging symbols for re-associating symbols with source code locations, if you need these features then recompile with `-g` to include debugging information with the executable.

\section{s:ocamlperf-conclusion}{Conclusion}

Enabling Frame Pointers may introduce a small performance penalty on certain architectures (up to 10% perf cost on x86_64 has been measured). Users of this feature are encouraged to do their own benchmarking to determine the impact.